{
  "id": "arima-101",
  "name": "Arima 101 · Getting Started with Arima Notebooks",
  "description": "A guided tour of Arima Notebooks itself — what it is, the Barista engine, cells & languages, running code, pipelines, packages, AI co-pilots, agents, and MCP. Start here.",
  "metadata": {
    "category": "tutorial",
    "language": "arima",
    "level": 101,
    "subcategory": "Getting Started"
  },
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "a101-intro",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "# Welcome to Arima Notebooks 👋\n\nArima Notebooks is a **local, browser-based, multi-language notebook for the agentic era** — a place where *you* and *AI agents* build on the same living code. This tour explains everything, in order. Press **▶ Guided** (in Settings → Tutorials) to have it read aloud, or just read along.\n\n**What makes Arima different:**\n- **Eight languages** in one notebook, switchable per cell.\n- **Pipelines** that chain cells into real workflows.\n- **AI co-pilots** and a full **MCP server** — agents are first-class users.\n- **Local-first** — nothing leaves your machine.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "a101-barista",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "## ☕ Why \"Barista\"?\n\nArima is built on **Java**. In a coffee bar, a *barista* serves what you order — and Arima's **Barista** engine is the pure-Java core that \"serves\" every capability: running your cells, executing pipelines, managing packages, and powering the MCP server.\n\n**Java is the coffee; Barista brews it.** So when you see `barista.table(...)` in a cell, or \"brewed by Barista,\" that's the engine at work.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "a101-cells",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "## 1. Cells\n\nA notebook is a stack of **cells**. Each cell is either **Markdown** (prose, like this) or **Code** (runnable). Add cells with **+ Code** / **+ Markdown** in the toolbar.\n\nRun a code cell with **▶** or **Shift+Enter**. Try the one below.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "a101-run",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "// Your first cell - it runs on Barista, Arima's Java/JShell engine.\n// JShell takes bare statements. There is no class to declare and no main method\n// to sit inside: what you write is the whole program.\nSystem.out.println(\"Hello from Arima!\");\n\n// Two int variables in one declaration. Names bound in a cell stay alive for the\n// rest of the session, so a cell you add below can still read a and b - that\n// shared state is what makes a notebook feel different from a script.\nint a = 6, b = 7;\n\n// Java has no string interpolation, so + does two jobs here. Because the leftmost\n// operand is already a String, each following + appends rather than adds. The\n// parentheses around a * b are load-bearing: without them the multiplication\n// would happen after the appends and you would get \"67\" instead of 42.\nSystem.out.println(a + \" x \" + b + \" = \" + (a * b));",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "a101-langs",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "## 2. Eight languages, one canvas\n\nEvery code cell has a **mode badge** (top-right). Click it to cycle through **JShell · Java · JavaScript · TypeScript · C# · F# · C++ · Python**. Each runs on its real toolchain — no language is a second-class citizen.\n\nPick the languages you care about in **Settings → Languages**; the tutorial list tailors itself to your choice.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "a101-barista-helpers",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "// Barista gives every cell display helpers. Here is a quick table plus stats.\n// In a JShell cell the helper is the BaristaDisplay class, imported for you when\n// the session starts - the lowercase `barista` object you will see in the\n// JavaScript, TypeScript and Python tutorials is those runtimes' equivalent.\n//\n// table() takes the rows as a String[][] and the column headers as the trailing\n// arguments. It emits HTML, so the output pane renders it as a real table\n// instead of printing the text.\nString[][] rows = {\n    { \"Python\", \"4.0\" },\n    { \"C++\",    \"3.0\" },\n    { \"JShell\", \"1.0\" },\n};\nBaristaDisplay.table(rows, \"lang\", \"since\");\n\n// stats() takes a label and a double[], then reports count, min, max, mean,\n// median and standard deviation in one call - the summary you usually want\n// first when a column of numbers lands in front of you.\nBaristaDisplay.stats(\"since\", new double[] { 4.0, 3.0, 1.0 });",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "a101-pipelines",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "## 3. Pipelines — Arima's superpower\n\nGive a cell a name with `//@ anchor: myStep`, then have another cell declare `//@ depends: myStep`. Arima builds a **dependency graph** and runs cells in the right order — a real, repeatable workflow that works **across all eight languages** and even **across notebooks**.\n\nAdd a **Pipeline** cell (toolbar) to orchestrate a whole graph, then **Run All**.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "a101-packages",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "## 4. Packages\n\nBring real libraries. The **Packages** tab has one manager per ecosystem:\n\n| Tab | For | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Maven** | Java / JShell | `com.google.code.gson:gson` |\n| **npm** | JavaScript / TypeScript | `lodash` |\n| **NuGet** | C# / F# | `Newtonsoft.Json` |\n| **PyPI** | Python | `pandas`, `requests` |\n\nInstalls are **local to you** and isolated. If you open a notebook that imports a package you don't have, Arima **prompts you to install it** — one click takes you to the right tab, pre-filled.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "a101-ai",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "## 5. AI co-pilots\n\nPress **Ctrl+\\\\** (or the floating ✨ button) to open the AI panel. **Claude**, **GitHub Copilot**, and **Antigravity** all run **locally — no API keys**. Ask the AI to generate a cell, explain an error, or convert code between languages, and apply the result with one click (Undo always available).\n\nOn the **Home** page, the ask box sends your question straight to the AI.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "a101-agents",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "## 6. Agents — and why Arima is \"agentic\"\n\nAn **agent** in Arima *is* a notebook (its markdown cells are the system prompt). Author and run agents & skills from the **Agents** tab, invoke an agent from a cell in a pipeline, or drive one over MCP.\n\n**MCP (Model Context Protocol):** Arima runs a built-in MCP server at `/api/mcp/sse`. Every notebook and cell is **tool-invokable by external agents** (Claude Desktop, Claude Code, custom loops) — `barista_list_notebooks`, `barista_run_pipeline`, `barista_run_agent`, and more. Humans and agents are equal users of the same notebook.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "a101-learn",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "mode": "jshell",
      "source": "## 7. Keep learning\n\n- **Tutorials** (Settings → Tutorials): per-language tracks 101 → 601. Press **▶ Guided** for a narrated, step-by-step walkthrough you can interrupt with questions.\n- **Home** (the Arima logo or Home tab): recent notebooks, the MCP catalog, what's new, and a quick quiz.\n- **Docs** (top bar): full reference, with a distraction-free reading mode.\n- **Notifications** (🔔): when a notebook finishes a Run All, click the bell to jump right back to it.\n\nThat's the tour — now go build something. Happy brewing! ☕",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    }
  ]
}
